    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. Evaluating the Offer\n\nOn paper, this is a respectable **standard baseline offer** for an L6 Product Manager at Google (where median total compensation typically falls between $520,000 and $580,000). However, given your specific situation, **it is not a compelling reason to leave Meta as-is**:\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Offer (Google L6) | Difference / Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Pay cut on guaranteed cash) |\n| **Target Bonus** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Equity (4-Yr Avg)** | ~$250,000 / yr | $270,000 / yr ($1.08M / 4) | **+$20,000 / yr** |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | No buffer for transition risk / unvested stock |\n| **Annualized TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+~5% increase** |\n\n#### Key Factors to Consider:\n1. **The \"Front-Loaded\" Equity Schedule:** Google new-hire grants typically do not vest evenly at 25% per year. They are usually front-loaded (either **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**). \n   - While this boosts your Year 1 cash flow (e.g., ~$356k\u2013$410k in stock Year 1), it creates an **equity cliff in Years 3 and 4**.\n   - Google relies on annual refreshers to fill that cliff, but as your recruiter noted, refreshes depend heavily on performance and market conditions.\n2. **You Hold Strong Leverage:** You are happy where you are, were actively recruited, and are coming from Meta (which Google calibrates as a direct peer). A 5% bump is not enough to take on the risk of resetting your internal reputation, performance ratings, and vesting momentum.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strategy: Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n**Negotiate.** \n\nDo not accept the first offer, and do not walk away without countering. Google almost never puts their top-of-band numbers in the initial offer sheet for Senior PMs. Because you are content staying at Meta, you have maximum leverage to ask for a top-of-band package.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What Exactly to Ask For & Target Numbers\n\nGoogle has flexibility on **base salary**, **sign-on bonus**, and **initial equity**.\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502 TARGET COUNTER-OFFER TARGETS (L6 PM SF)                \u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n\u2502 Component             \u2502 Initial Offer  \u2502 Target Ask    \u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n\u2502 Base Salary           \u2502 $235,000       \u2502 $255,000      \u2502\n\u2502 Sign-on Bonus         \u2502 $0             \u2502 $80,000       \u2502\n\u2502 Equity (4-Yr GSU)     \u2502 $1,080,000     \u2502 $1,280,000    \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n#### Breakdown of Requests:\n* **Base Salary ($255,000):** Google\u2019s SF range goes up to $271,000. It makes no sense to take a base pay cut from $240,000 to $235,000. Asking for $255k puts you comfortably within the upper third of their band.\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($75,000 \u2013 $80,000):** Google frequently uses one-time sign-on bonuses to bridge the gap for candidates leaving unvested equity and upcoming bonuses at peer companies.\n* **Equity ($1,280,000 / $320k avg/yr):** Pushing the equity grant from $1.08M to ~$1.28M places the package at the 75th+ percentile for L6 PMs in the Bay Area.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Frame the Counter-Offer (Script)\n\nReach out to the recruiter via phone (or email if preferred) with a collaborative, appreciative, but firm tone:\n\n> *\"Thank you for walking me through the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of the role and the impact I could have at Google.*\n>\n> *As you know, I wasn\u2019t actively looking to leave Meta because I\u2019m in a great spot with strong momentum. Looking closely at the numbers, the current base is slightly below what I currently earn ($240k), and the overall annualized compensation is nearly lateral to my current trajectory when factoring in my unvested equity.*\n> \n> *To make the decision straightforward and offset the friction of leaving my existing vesting pipeline, I\u2019d be ready to sign if we can get to:*\n> - *A base salary of **$255,000***\n> - *A 4-year equity grant of **$1,280,000***\n> - *A sign-on bonus of **$80,000***\n>\n> *If Google can meet these numbers, I\u2019m ready to accept and submit my notice.\"*\n\n### Key Clarification to Ask Before Signing\nAsk the recruiter to confirm the **exact vesting cadence** for the initial grant (e.g., standard 33/33/22/12 vs. 38/32/20/10) and whether vesting is monthly or quarterly. This ensures you can accurately map out your exact Year 1 and Year 2 cash flows.",
