    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line: **Yes, you should strongly consider accepting this offer.**\n\nGoogle delivered a major concession here. By adding a **$200,000 sign-on bonus** and matching your base salary, they addressed your unvested equity risk and pushed the package into top-of-market territory for an L6 PM.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Math: Google vs. Meta\n\nGoogle grants equity on a front-loaded schedule (typically **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%**). \n\nUsing the standard **33/33/22/12** schedule, here is how the cash flow compares:\n\n| Year | Meta (Estimated) | Google (New Offer) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | ~$526,000 | **~$844,400** <br><small>($240k base + $48k bonus + $356.4k stock + $200k sign-on)</small> | **+$318,400** |\n| **Year 2** | ~$526,000 | **~$644,400** <br><small>($240k base + $48k bonus + $356.4k stock)</small> | **+$118,400** |\n| **Year 3** | ~$526,000 | **~$525,600 + Refreshes** <br><small>($240k base + $48k bonus + $237.6k stock)</small> | Flat (before refreshes) |\n| **Year 4** | ~$526,000 | **~$417,600 + Refreshes** <br><small>($240k base + $48k bonus + $129.6k stock)</small> | Depends on refreshers |\n| **2-Year Total** | **~$1,052,000** | **~$1,488,800** | **+$436,800 (+41.5%)** |\n\n*(If they use the 38/32/20/10 schedule, your Year 1 jumps even higher to roughly **~$898,000**).*\n\n#### Why this is a win:\n* **The $200k \"walk-away\" unvested equity is replaced immediately in liquid cash** rather than waiting 12 months for it to vest at Meta.\n* **You earn roughly $430k\u2013$450k more over the next 24 months** compared to staying at Meta.\n* The sign-on bonus completely de-risks the friction of leaving your current team and resetting your reputation.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Can You Negotiate Further?\n\n**No.** When Google increases an offer by over $200,000 in total value and explicitly states it is their best/final offer, it almost certainly is. \n\nPushing again at this point risks creating friction with the hiring manager and the compensation committee without yielding any meaningful upside.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Non-Financial Factors to Weigh Before Signing\n\nSince you are happy at Meta, evaluate these cultural and structural dynamics:\n\n1. **Product Execution vs. Consensus:**\n   * **Meta (IC6):** Move-fast culture, highly data-driven, bottom-up execution with significant autonomy and fast launch cycles.\n   * **Google (L6):** More consensus-driven, heavier stakeholder management, and cross-org alignment. PMs often spend more time on strategy, stakeholder alignment, and documentation.\n2. **Path to L7 (Principal PM / Director Track):**\n   * Promoting from L6 to L7 at Google can take time and requires broad organizational scope and visibility. Ensure the team and product area you are joining have clear executive sponsorship and charter.\n3. **The Year 3/4 Cliff:**\n   * Your base + initial equity will drop in Year 3 and Year 4. To maintain a $600k+ run-rate past Year 2, you will need solid performance ratings to stack annual equity refreshes.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Confirm Before You Sign\n\nAsk the recruiter for the formal written offer and confirm these two details:\n1. **Sign-On Payment Schedule & Clawback Terms:** Ensure the $200,000 is paid in your first or second paycheck, and verify the clawback period (typically 12 months pro-rated or lump-sum repayment if you leave voluntarily within Year 1).\n2. **Vesting Schedule Breakdown:** Confirm whether it follows the 33/33/22/12 or 38/32/20/10 schedule, and whether vesting is monthly or quarterly.\n\n### Verdict\n**Take the offer.** You successfully extracted top-of-market terms that guarantee you a transformative financial upside in Years 1 and 2 while having both Meta and Google L6/IC6 credentials on your resume.",
